<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>DemoProducts.Consumer</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      No PublishAot here, deliberately: this host ships as a framework-dependent worker. The reason in
      ADR 0001 was the Avro DESERIALIZE path (Avro.SpecificDefaultReader -> Avro.ObjectCreator), which
      resolves record types BY NAME through Activator. That reason now applies only when
      Kafka:Consumer:EnableAvroReader is true; the generic reader this sample ships by default does not
      go through ObjectCreator. IsAotCompatible still applies from Directory.Build.props, so the
      source-level analysers keep running over this project either way.

      A native publish IS supported as an opt-in and has been verified end to end: publish this project
      for linux-x64, self-contained, with PublishAot set to true. The README carries the full command.

      The group below is what makes PublishAot the only flag that command needs. It applies ONLY when
      PublishAot is on, so the default framework-dependent build is untouched.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true'">
    <!--
      Same demotion as the Api, and for the same reason: TreatWarningsAsErrors propagates into ILC, which
      reports 60 third-party diagnostics as errors. They come from Confluent.SchemaRegistry, Avro and
      Newtonsoft.Json - the read path keeps Confluent's serde on purpose - and NONE from DemoProducts.*.
      TrimmerSingleWarn=false is what makes them name their methods instead of collapsing into one
      IL2104 per assembly.
    -->
    <IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Same mechanism as the Api, and only relevant when PublishAot is on: ILC forwards $(NoWarn) into its
    own nowarn argument. Without it the opt-in native publish prints 60 third-party diagnostics.

    The list is wider than the Api's because this host keeps Confluent's Avro serde and Schema Registry
    client on purpose, and those reach Newtonsoft.Json and Avro's reflective ObjectCreator. The origins
    are Confluent.SchemaRegistry, Confluent.SchemaRegistry.Serdes, Avro, Newtonsoft.Json and
    Confluent.Kafka. NONE come from DemoProducts.*, which is the number that actually matters.

    The width is also why this is scoped to a target that runs before ILC and gated on PublishAot: it
    must not reach the C# compiler, and it must not touch the ordinary framework-dependent build at all.

        dotnet publish ... -p:PublishAot=true -p:ShowThirdPartyTrimWarnings=true

    brings all 60 back. Read ADR 0001 before widening the list.
  -->
  <Target Name="SilenceThirdPartyIlcWarnings"
          BeforeTargets="WriteIlcRspFileForCompilation"
          Condition="'$(ShowThirdPartyTrimWarnings)' != 'true'">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL2055;IL2057;IL2067;IL2072;IL2075;IL2091;IL3050</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- Inert unless this project is published with PublishAot=true; see the file for what it buys. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <TrimmerRootDescriptor Include="ILLink.Descriptors.xml" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DemoProducts.Application\DemoProducts.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DemoProducts.Infrastructure\DemoProducts.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Extensions.Hosting" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Microsoft.NET.Sdk does not copy appsettings.json the way the Web SDK does. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="appsettings.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="appsettings.Development.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
